AI critique log that records, claim by claim, what you kept, corrected, and removed after verification. A good prompt gets you a useful draft; verification turns that draft into something you can stand behind. Without the verification step, you are not using AI, you are repeating it.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Write a prompt specific enough to produce checkable claims, and ask the model to flag uncertainty.\n- Separate the model's output into individual factual claims you can verify one at a time.\n- Confirm, correct, or remove each claim against an official source, and log the result.\n- Recognize a hallucination: a confident, specific claim with no source behind it.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nPriya needs three campus facts for a \"new student guide.\" She asks a chatbot and gets a clean list: the Writing Center is on the second floor of the library, the Health Center is open 24/7, and there are free laptop loans at the Student Union in room 210. The list looks authoritative. But when she checks, the Health Center's posted hours are 8 AM to 6 PM, and there is no room 210 laptop program anywhere on the university site.\n\nTwo of the three facts were wrong, and nothing in the AI's tone warned her. If she had pasted the list straight into her guide, she would have published confident misinformation under her own name.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nVerification works because of a key property of language models: fluency and accuracy are independent. The model is optimized to produce text that sounds right, not text that is right. A made-up room number and a real room number are written with identical confidence. Therefore you cannot judge a claim by how it reads; you must judge it by whether a trustworthy source confirms it.\n\nThe reliable procedure is a **prompt-then-verify loop** with three moves.\n\n| Move | What you do | Why it matters |\n|------|-------------|----------------|\n| Constrain the prompt | Ask for specifics and request uncertainty flags | Specific claims are checkable; vague claims hide errors |\n| Atomize the output | Split the answer into separate claims | You verify one fact at a time, not a paragraph |\n| Verify each claim | Confirm, correct, or remove against a source | Only checked facts survive into your work |\n\nThe strongest single tactic is to ask the model to mark anything it is unsure about. Models will often flag their own shaky claims if invited, which tells you where to aim your checking first. But the flag is a hint, not a guarantee; an unflagged claim still needs verification.\n\n<inline-check question=\"AI returns three perfectly formatted, peer-reviewed citations with authors, journals, years, and page numbers. Why is this the most dangerous case?\" explanation=\"Fabricated citations are a model specialty: the format is real even when the paper is not. The polished formatting is no evidence of truth, so each title must resolve to a real, accessible source.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Clean formatting proves the citations are real\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The format can be flawless while the papers do not exist, so each must be searched and confirmed\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Citations never need checking once they are formatted in APA\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Only the page numbers can be wrong\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nThe weak version below sounds plausible, but it does not yet give a reader enough evidence:\n\n> I asked AI and it gave me three campus facts.\n\nThat sentence is too thin because it does not show that any fact was checked. A reader has no way to know whether the claims are true. A strong record shows the original output, the source you checked each claim against, and what you did with it.\n\n### Worked Example: prompt, then verify, step by step\n\nYou need three campus facts for a \"new student guide.\" Run a prompt-then-verify loop.\n\n1. **Write a specific prompt:** `Act as a campus guide. List three services for first-year students (name, location, one-line purpose). Mark any item you are not certain about.`\n2. **Read the output and flag claims.** AI returns: (a) Writing Center, Library 2nd floor; (b) Health Center, open 24/7; (c) \"Free laptop loans at the Student Union, room 210.\"\n3. **Verify each against an official source.** (a) Confirmed on the library site: kept. (b) Official hours say 8 AM to 6 PM, not 24/7: corrected to the real hours. (c) No room 210 laptop loan program found anywhere: removed as likely fabricated.\n4. **Record the verification:** `Claim a: confirmed (library site). Claim b: corrected hours (health center page). Claim c: removed, unverifiable.`\n\nResult: one claim kept, one corrected, one cut. The guide now contains only facts you checked, with a trail a reader can follow.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Verify three campus facts, one claim at a time\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"AI returns: \\\"Writing Center, Library 2nd floor.\\\" The library website confirms this exact location. What is the verification result?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Keep on confidence: it sounds right\",\"Confirm against the source and keep it\",\"Remove it: AI is never reliable\",\"Correct it even though the source agrees\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Confirmed against the library site, so it is kept. A claim survives only after an authoritative source agrees with it.\"},{\"prompt\":\"AI says \\\"Health Center, open 24/7,\\\" but the official page posts hours of 8 AM to 6 PM. What do you do?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Keep the 24/7 claim: it is more helpful\",\"Correct it to the posted 8 AM to 6 PM hours\",\"Remove the Health Center entirely\",\"Trust whichever sounds more convenient\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Correct it. The source contradicts the AI, so you replace the fabricated hours with the verified ones.\"},{\"prompt\":\"AI claims \\\"free laptop loans at the Student Union, room 210,\\\" but no official page mentions any such program. What do you log?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Keep it: room 210 is very specific\",\"Remove it as unverifiable, likely fabricated\",\"Correct the room number to 211\",\"Confirm it because the wording is confident\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Remove it. A confident, specific claim with no source behind it is a hallucination, and oddly specific details are exactly what models fabricate most convincingly.\"},{\"reveal\":\"One kept, one corrected, one removed. Fluency and accuracy are independent, so each factual claim is a hypothesis until a source confirms it.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\nOrder the loop so verification becomes automatic.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Write a specific prompt and ask the model to flag uncertainty\",\"Split the output into separate, checkable claims\",\"Find an official or primary source for each claim\",\"Confirm, correct, or remove each claim against the source\",\"Log the source and the decision for every claim\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nTreat any specific, factual claim from AI as unverified until a primary or official source confirms it. Numbers, names, dates, locations, hours, prices, and citations are the highest-risk items because they are exactly what models fabricate most convincingly. If you cannot find a source for a claim, you do not get to keep it because it \"sounds right\"; you correct it or remove it.\n\n1. **Source check:** Is there an official page, document, or record that confirms this claim?\n2. **Specificity check:** Did you verify the exact number, date, or location, not just the general idea?\n3. **Removal check:** For any claim you could not confirm, did you correct or delete it?\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most damaging misreading is \"the AI was confident, so it was probably right.\" Confidence is a property of the writing style, not of the truth. A fabricated citation is formatted as carefully as a real one. The smoother the lie, the more important the check.\n\nA second misreading is verifying the easy claims and trusting the hard ones. Students often confirm a claim they could already guess and skip the unfamiliar fact, which is the one most likely to be wrong. Aim your verification at the claims you cannot already vouch for, especially anything flagged or anything oddly specific.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nImagine using AI to help with a citation list for a short paper. You ask for \"three peer-reviewed sources on study habits.\" The model returns three entries with authors, journals, years, and page numbers, all perfectly formatted. This is the most dangerous case, because fabricated citations are the model's specialty: the format is real even when the paper is not. Verify by searching each title in the library database or Google Scholar. Two resolve to real articles; one returns nothing, and the \"journal\" does not exist. You remove the third and replace it with a real source you found and read.\n\nThe walkthrough shows verification is not optional even when the output looks scholarly. The fix is procedural, not clever: split the list, search each entry, keep only what resolves to a real, accessible source, and log what you removed. A reader who opens your citation list should be able to find every source you kept, because you already did.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nWhen you read AI-generated text, train yourself to underline every specific, checkable claim: a number, a name, a date, a location, a quotation. Those underlines are your verification to-do list. Vague sentences (\"study habits are important\") need no source; specific ones (\"students who sleep 8 hours score 12% higher\") demand one before you repeat them.\n\nA strong reader of AI output can answer four questions:\n\n1. Which claims here are specific enough to be true or false?\n2. Which of those have I confirmed against a source?\n3. Which claim is the model itself flagging as uncertain?\n4. What did I correct or remove, and why?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nYour verification log is strong when a skeptic could re-check every kept claim using the sources you listed and reach the same conclusions. The earlier layers are the constrained prompt, the atomized claims, and the per-claim verification; the last layer is wording. If a claim is hard to verify, mark it in your trace and ask BigAL to help you locate an authoritative source rather than asking it to reassure you that the claim is fine.\n\nA useful note would read: \"AI gave three campus facts. I confirmed the Writing Center location on the library site, corrected the Health Center hours from a fabricated 24/7 to the posted 8 to 6, and removed the room 210 laptop claim because no official source mentions it.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nCarry the loop into a spreadsheet task where AI suggests a formula or a data value. The same independence between fluency and accuracy applies: AI will confidently propose `=VLOOKUP(...)` syntax that is subtly wrong, or assert that a column's total is a certain number. You verify a formula by running it on a row you can check by hand, and you verify a claimed total by recomputing it with `=SUM()`. The medium changed from facts to formulas, but the discipline did not: a confident output is a hypothesis, and you keep only what you have independently confirmed. Once verification is a reflex, it protects you in writing, research, data work, and anywhere else a tool hands you a fluent answer.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/01-04-prompting-with-verification-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"01.04\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show the raw AI output. The revised version should show which claims you confirmed, corrected, and removed, with the source for each. The trace should explain how you spotted the unverifiable claim.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare an unverified AI list with the same list after per-claim checking.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the prompt-then-verify loop to a new kind of factual claim.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes which claim you check first.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are building a verification log for CAT 100 01.04. A peer comment says one kept claim has no source listed and may be a hallucination. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would confirm, correct, or remove the claim.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Concept Map\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"prompting_practice\", \"ai_supported_learning\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"cat100_supplement_digital_citizenship_and_ai_readiness\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What best shows transfer rather than completion?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The learner explains how the same decision rule changes under a new audience or constraint.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"1\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_prompting_with_verification\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your log lists each AI claim, the source you checked it against, and whether you confirmed, corrected, or removed it, with nothing kept on confidence alone.\n\n## References\n\n- [NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)\n- [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students)\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- [WCAG accessibility](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/)\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "cat100_supplement_1_4_prompting_with_verification_artifact_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_prompting_with_verification", "concept_ids": ["prompting_practice", "cat100_supplement_digital_citizenship_and_ai_readiness"], "stem": "An AI answer states confidently: \"The library closes at 9 PM on weekends.\" You need this for an assignment. What is the right next step?", "options": ["Use it directly; the answer is clearly stated.", "Check the library's official hours page before relying on the time, because fluent wording is not proof of accuracy.", "Ask the AI again to see if it repeats the same time.", "Add the time to your work and note that AI is usually right."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Confident phrasing does not make a claim true. Verify factual details against a trustworthy source such as the official page.", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_1_4_prompting_with_verification_confident_means_correct", "difficulty": "easy"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_1_4_prompting_with_verification_annotation_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_prompting_with_verification", "concept_ids": ["prompting_practice"], "stem": "Which prompt is most likely to produce output you can actually verify and revise?", "options": ["Write about study skills.", "Act as a peer tutor. List three time-management tips for a first-year student studying 10 hours a week, each in one sentence, and mark which ones you are unsure about.", "Tell me something useful.", "Make this good."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "A strong prompt names the role, task, audience, format, and even asks the model to flag uncertainty, all of which make verification easier.", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_1_4_prompting_with_verification_vague_prompt", "difficulty": "medium"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_1_4_prompting_with_verification_ai_boundary", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_prompting_with_verification", "concept_ids": ["prompting_practice", "ai_supported_learning"], "stem": "An AI gives you a perfect-looking citation: \"Reed, J. (2019). Digital Habits. Journal of Campus Life, 12(4), 33-50.\" You search the library and the web and cannot find this article anywhere. What do you do?", "options": ["Cite it anyway; the format looks correct.", "Do not use the claim or the citation, because an unverifiable source may be fabricated.", "Change the year to 2020 and cite it.", "Ask the AI to confirm the citation is real and trust the reply."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "AI can invent realistic but fake citations. If you cannot locate the real source, drop both the citation and the claim it supports.", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_1_4_prompting_with_verification_fake_citation", "difficulty": "hard"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_1_4_prompting_with_verification_calibration", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_evidence_based_revision", "concept_ids": ["evidence_based_revision"], "stem": "You used AI to draft a paragraph with two factual claims. Which verification record best supports a defensible artifact?", "options": ["A note that says \"checked with AI.\"", "For each claim: the source you checked, whether it confirmed or contradicted the claim, and what you kept or cut.", "The total number of words the AI wrote.", "A screenshot of the AI chat with no notes."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "A defensible verification record ties each claim to a ch